From: perf session 2026-09-11
Subject: [PATCH 0025] wasm: halt-path costs - no host-clock reads in the icount budget, WFI without a longjmp

Two per-halt costs found by profiling the display-DMA stretch after
0023/0024 (vCPU self time, 12 s window):
  - _emscripten_get_now + BigInt wrappers 8 %: icount_get_limit() reads
    the QEMU_CLOCK_REALTIME deadline (a JS clock import, ~3 us) twice per
    vCPU loop round (icount_percpu_budget + icount_prepare_for_run), and
    icount_start_warp_timer read QEMU_CLOCK_VIRTUAL_RT before knowing it
    only needs it in the sleep=on branch.  On wasm the realtime timers run
    on the main-loop worker, which paces its own futex wait on them, so
    the budget term is dropped there (#ifndef __EMSCRIPTEN__); the warp
    timer read moves into its branch (generic).
  - __emscripten_throw_longjmp 2.8 %: helper_wfi's cpu_loop_exit is a JS
    exception unwind (~15 us) per WFI; WFI always ends its TB with
    exit_tb(0) and cpu_handle_interrupt delivers a pending exception_index
    before running anything else (0013's path), so on wasm the helper just
    sets halted/EXCP_HLT and returns.

Measured with 0026 stacked on 0023+0024 (idlebench --quick, one pair):
  dist-jit t0.75G 43.6->40.6 (-7 %), t1.3G 47.9->43.8 (-9 %)
Gates: see 0023 (stack).

